INSERT INTO `attractions` VALUES (1,'National Gallery Singapore','1 St Andrew\'s Road','Housed in the beautifully restored former Supreme Court and City Hall buildings, the National Gallery Singapore is home to the world’s largest public collection of modern Southeast Asian art. Visitors can explore over 8,000 works that reflect the region’s cultural evolution, while enjoying panoramic views of the Civic District from the rooftop garden. The museum’s architecture seamlessly fuses colonial grandeur with contemporary design, making it a landmark of both art and heritage.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,' http://www.nationalgallery.sg','Effective from 24 November 2015, Sunday to Thursday and  public holidays,10am - 7pm ,Friday to Saturday and  eve of public holidays, 10am - 10pm','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1760801718915_National_Gallery_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=Cfq4pybFrNh6iJ2gRtlgqoRgudzeNh68kaQoM1m76ta%2BWDLMYDRAxuSxaKLWBPoJkuRqNyqSjN%2F2dprvG5suSLahqZ0z6C%2Fiq1YpQh75wi0eSAU1vCe9PBnT%2BMtMJyeqJU8ZYQ5X%2FEzrwKXtlS7EiNJv6Kv48yAVGLs1w%2BLukxyHnmt5LIIlmEn9zuFpXnaSZa7yu70eTwJgqUoziUZtjMIWDecZhvKfdkFTKMMm0UV1wtxPSfWoIwT9QvlC%2BegPyRtRq6P0UyrLuzAlGVo3TvH6jjPGdhqqEGE3dpaKsX7ZcWe6oUGkxmWzWBsDDLBlcFp5r%2FKterDO2HI9jaHhBQ%3D%3D','Central / Civic District\r'),(2,'Sultan Mosque (Masjid Sultan) Singapore','3 Muscat Street','Built in 1824 for Sultan Hussein Shah, the Sultan Mosque stands as the spiritual heart of Kampong Glam. Its grand golden domes, towering minarets, and intricate mosaic details draw visitors from around the world. The mosque remains a focal point for Singapore’s Muslim community, welcoming visitors to experience its serene prayer halls and rich history. It’s one of the nation’s most photographed and revered religious landmarks. ','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'http://www.sultanmosque.org.sg','Monday to Sunday:9.30am - 12pm and 2pm - 4pm ,Friday:2.30pm - 4pm','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1763046785328_Sultan_Mosque_%28Masjid_Sultan%29_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=XrwkQCNkGtMCO2DocxjMYG9KgQOKdegNUY3fD2vmZzGBPJd36Fi5imsC4ehL0jbBBSAKzzuBVSbh9yphRt3PS9SIHLGVyq%2FzT96MOdQyne4YdQiA9cPaEsJCI5wZPekgSSgC8enRAYvW0woGwpKLIF98sNrfhASnglmvL51JLxnIutyWGkqJ6CQAO495a%2FmUYiGp%2F2hC%2FhlLbdVGPSCb8sSyA20XxWnqYRstVgY8kncLF%2FGDv%2Br5oDGLOPX4NdNT3%2F%2B9R1of2%2FN4HGKbF6JxaW%2B0cpm1oScAe13LV9j7VXkxn%2FdSRKTbNW1FAqLad%2F1IDJrpK15Fh2vpc6iICvbeZQ%3D%3D','General Singapore\r'),(3,'Sri Mariamman Temple: Hindu Temple in Singapore','244 South Bridge Road','Founded in 1827, Sri Mariamman Temple is Singapore’s oldest Hindu temple, dedicated to the goddess Mariamman, known for her healing powers. Located in the heart of Chinatown, its ornate gopuram (tower) is adorned with colorful sculptures depicting Hindu deities and mythological scenes. The temple serves as a cultural and spiritual center for the Hindu community and hosts the annual fire-walking festival, Thimithi.','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'http://heb.gov.sg/our-subsidiaries/temples/sri-mariamman-temple.aspx','Daily from 7am -12pm, and 6pm - 9pm','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340629111_Sri_Mariamman_Temple%3A_Hindu_Temple_in_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=TKdIOdjmfn2Ppv892c%2BxWVFwaYRfSwsf%2FQgYWrud9dAjZIcY%2FVWIJLOEogjIOMp5hBapeYf5h5Cu7RVIIsiGkbpoGhy8FnXcXtvK6cHGjdZ9MrUkk7LQwjP4BeHDfUAngEDFzV0O7ZDpBo%2BSP4SWseSfOdBRy5uUqlvtWlV0zm7JTebay21zEjA0LvjYSCkkswNYLi0xFmmLM%2BHI67DFDvNa4a469qPFZWcKNfungmrVO9BmBzD2tLP5oA82wAxQC8goZ8vqIl%2BZRDOeUg0G8VNpMI%2BgfTBnSjHZ7Fohtq%2FSoJicqRM5%2Fb%2FTtJco2T65VC4HhQ07Sj9m71G1%2F876lw%3D%3D','Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(4,'Armenian Church in Singapore','60 Hill Street','The Armenian Church of St. Gregory the Illuminator, completed in 1835, is Singapore’s oldest Christian church. Designed by colonial architect George Coleman, it features neoclassical architecture, whitewashed walls, and tranquil gardens. Once serving the small Armenian community, the church is now a national monument and a peaceful sanctuary amid the city’s bustle. Its grounds include memorials to notable Armenians who shaped Singapore’s early history.','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'http://www.armeniansinasia.org/','Daily, 9am â€“6pm','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340638480_Armenian_Church_in_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=EvIkRYPHbYT2DAIHNDhwNiVVvmxhovP1mVoIOhAKqziybXnfPogohzVxaslo39f9RzoXxFTDAbZT6MKaVRJC8t1STidGz%2FxQxyON%2FyE7f4%2FujatxfblwQPxlr6GOzr%2FvSOR5CuiXjsQoaJC0HlGBrsiETggqwwKDsgr3Xm%2FHUx6btF2SwEiagf0WjhZw4zl5fuTR%2FvYpfEJ83GBCjcH8qdRo%2BGIxvI3RXrXTswrCEPEFth4UkCpDbstmZa4UyuKzFmp%2FYhQKBOAj6ooHAaNi%2FfCY%2F9aKTxjzqHa73pDEDXlEx3t0kJB1RrH25v2w%2BzofjJN78pMJ2pH1Gq9%2BrfzJxA%3D%3D','Central / Civic District\r'),(5,'CHIJMES Singapore','30 Victoria Street','Once a Catholic convent school dating back to the mid-19th century, CHIJMES (Convent of the Holy Infant Jesus Middle Education School) is now a vibrant lifestyle destination filled with restaurants, bars, and event spaces. Its beautifully preserved Gothic chapel and courtyards make it a popular venue for weddings, concerts, and cultural events. The blend of heritage architecture and modern use captures Singapore’s spirit of adaptive reuse.','Landmarks & Iconic Sites',1,'http://chijmes.com.sg/','Daily, 24 hours,Opening hours of businesses in CHIJMES may vary.','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340647120_CHIJMES_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=tu2yWmLI96wzWPVo7NcPQ8SVzzRg%2BjPKB41WA5ONFnnUhLEErFSX7uzEJlkC9HRzyppxKXo5qBCG86ka0%2FGvTRFvxxF5JBVmDjjgNKSt29qIEanJF1WaGukin9vxsiO3tQfBZ7STEkn8iBCwrUDiB3YF4dxwh8%2BvH9cOlvaMjQswBnPp2oVvb%2BwRU0Mr0uwcINEPtaAGgbdQlaz4PzFgRbwVs4IkKua4DYx1E9uvmv3wEKSqE1OwVxAC2mJuR%2BXM1Ppiau6ZlFardAAMAmSlJ%2F4zd7LNGaoBk9pmhoMP6G1GNsDygVuxQ5fJTKNUQIHH3n9MQ8JuSuCUO0iCpk744w%3D%3D','Orchard / Bras Basah\r'),(6,'St Andrew\'s Cathedral- Singapore Architecture Landmark','11 St Andrew\'s Road','As Singapore’s largest and oldest Anglican cathedral, St Andrew’s Cathedral was consecrated in 1862 and remains a striking example of Neo-Gothic architecture. Its gleaming white spires and stained-glass windows are instantly recognizable in the Civic District skyline. The cathedral continues to host regular services and community outreach, embodying both spiritual devotion and colonial architectural elegance.','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'http://www.livingstreams.org.sg/sac/visitors.html','Monday to Saturday, 9am â€“ 5pm','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340657133_St_Andrew%27s_Cathedral-_Singapore_Architecture_Landmark?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=UABYdUQeLcBlS5%2FwlYzCz2dKAZC1Rn1feQJbOeo2gawVlu2Rtt12ysmQB%2FRFJZ78Uqmoz%2F6m4r3231SDIZP4ALUtPhMvPPALfik%2B%2BjNV10r0%2BhCavowYKs8v8linowuiwhJSYi8ZXhSpIgOm%2Bgxct8VigBbRJtyWGGl5FcPSR4bLVQnRv48G4Nh9tB45izzzN4lq3RZBfWqDYqnYKP%2FBtqZMlF4bKoCWgeloZiNNxYosNzGoj%2BWIjWqubpS21CMldGLb3ZKRe5QRHdHqU7nY3TK8UhO8cFCv7IHKCPFad5L1HvuZ4bIb5%2FsEI3lmN9IXugy3StazFDNckTIJbfkoCA%3D%3D','General Singapore'),(7,'Kreta Ayer Square','Banda Street, 050005','Situated in the heart of Chinatown, Kreta Ayer Square serves as a cultural stage for traditional Chinese opera and festive celebrations. It’s surrounded by shophouses, market stalls, and eateries that capture the old-world charm of the district. The square comes alive during major festivals like Chinese New Year, offering visitors a glimpse into the community’s living heritage.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'','','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340669966_Kreta_Ayer_Square?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=qG9%2BOWXHWQtKTIsojFMt9ug9YWsZ627%2BBHhOsH0jy0YBDhd9BsRIvpPI5DEVgx9TiMr9L%2BkpPCEl%2BQr%2B4osnjQHO%2Ft9Fzj11uoNVhjhr4kNuI3kdIlhxUvknP3SecDrhcQ28k2g1vcnGtQhX%2FDMpMA97ZHdHWGlqe9scq%2F1byB5nE%2Fvw04TIbesgoxsGY70NbeJP%2BbeCYYB%2BFd3Mhe%2FTot2wuGkUmwPUzoMCJjnYQTFtouRXHO5ePumOzsQjwqYtSdpns1L2ZVt7iEOCypIjFc5lWX97xTVRekMa4vijLMKTIlqLXXTD4VV2iNtTWIfaoHXaljZJxVOknBO%2BBjj52Q%3D%3D','Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(8,'Albert Mall Trishaw Park ','Queen Street, 188542','Albert Mall Trishaw Park, located between Bugis and Rochor, is a nostalgic reminder of Singapore’s past. It’s the central hub for trishaw rides that take visitors through historic neighborhoods. Elderly trishaw riders, many of whom have been in the trade for decades, share personal stories as they pedal through the city’s evolving landscape. The park itself features murals and rest areas celebrating this heritage trade.','Parks & Nature',2,'','','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340681129_Albert_Mall_Trishaw_Park_?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=FRE9HWYofAL0YSseqa00LsNysTxSzFBave01Nvm7JZO%2FqlWyIVAr1Ik6FctKjjev9aI%2Fo6pwGFy4J47utFIxn8cO2zZteY9o5qXhiHsrJc8uNr9oKTyg%2Bj4lhGr0BGWjtvWQMtSaKaWec1Nuy%2FBuhNp%2BrHkL50I4cdBU75yb9NJ5WB4RhqffYaGXKOCZ1Lqtbr3swpc2c%2FassoMUN%2BnB3%2Bc2Pr9jaQHfiQ%2FaZ9qEiIKfnb6Sdhpru0dfDIqds6f0iEe9Qw4iiBL5Vl9QTJSvvaRq7QuO5FsLI7xUDkdeKGUGK7fFXfTxeXd21fiFSlR2rSprWWbf3025uQDdya9Hgg%3D%3D','General Singapore\r'),(9,'Chinatown Food Street ','Smith Street, 058938','Chinatown Food Street is a culinary hotspot that celebrates Singapore’s hawker heritage under a sheltered, lantern-lit street. Visitors can sample iconic local dishes like satay, char kway teow, and Hainanese chicken rice from traditional stalls. By blending nostalgia with modern comfort, the street offers both locals and tourists an authentic taste of Singapore’s multicultural cuisine.','Food & Culinary',2,'','','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340688250_Chinatown_Food_Street_?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=MLsIbb4JScYDN12aRG6z3WU5JTQgI3ef1AWHHcOF8NB1L7fJvMSWxpoR906gKxfCF%2FoMXJ%2FCLOAkGl56nLoXuqeWRvG5fqPPP542mA2Iv7zqEhLPDgwcb1iBaP5FWPVts1UkwmXdWsy%2FfbWDWjpsuyZJGzy02G1TinO9vNhRrKYvCkRWJTJ41mZ8JqaCNeoY4j8Qs%2F0VhKjFxaKMAmbBMXEUTcfiQ3g2tcYS0ZioLU5Ibf4v1PCasqlqJQit0taoyj7ZlNfurDnM2Qn3VMVuqU%2FTSFEFU1ZFa6a0IxUdG1QuFV8nNaz7Xvr219FlODSfxGi0Ci4d%2Bw54fqTPZOaUNQ%3D%3D','Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(10,'Chinatown Heritage Centre, Singapore','48 Pagoda Street','Located within restored shophouses on Pagoda Street, the Chinatown Heritage Centre recreates the cramped living quarters and immigrant stories of early Chinese settlers. The museum offers immersive exhibits, period artifacts, and multimedia storytelling that vividly bring Singapore’s migrant past to life. It’s an essential stop for understanding how Chinatown evolved into today’s cultural heartland.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'','Daily, 9am â€“ 8pm,Last entry at 7pm.*Chinatown Heritage Centre will be closed for renovation from 1 October 2014 till end-2015.','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340696497_Chinatown_Heritage_Centre%2C_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=FRMPRsRwh630jpwr2c1VshuNpqsD41xrzaD3KYGxBytqtmKdz193bgMIzBw%2BZyrSgVDglmUqnqxwzFJly%2BV4mcr8j6xPShg40wdR562UMwpUwSuC7mHZBcApHxBNZAhfNyX3jdkbD2CwjK9nY4m4uu4L6Cug6q1Rx9AQ9fLMTgTlOdnM3uJFhHXCzDe2DL6Mrs46GgPvejoIbeimNyY4LMZoA0PqRU2fdkpLcJxoP87VU2KPjmq92VB%2BmwuJ7PoP%2FvLQq0%2FyxUDeazmjibGElw%2BSZ0Wn4apKHrCePTunfcUXBl3yvBQLlWe5AP90uJalhot9gUY%2Bk%2FT9gk%2B3B0jehg%3D%3D','Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(11,'Thian Hock Keng Temple, Singapore','158 Telok Ayer Street','Built in 1842 by Hokkien seafarers, Thian Hock Keng Temple is one of Singapore’s oldest and most important Hokkien temples. Dedicated to Mazu, the Goddess of the Sea, its intricate carvings, porcelain figurines, and southern Chinese architecture were constructed without a single nail. The temple remains an active place of worship and a symbol of maritime heritage.','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'http://www.thianhockkeng.com.sg','Daily, 7.30am â€“ 5.30pm','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340711400_Thian_Hock_Keng_Temple%2C_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=cgUGuxDbofFgZ51JmPvpR8bOC9Kuy7szosbdtR3oqHS3L70moJ97%2FD8JMMRuiz28ONo6duF0o682zwrnXkYu1UlJ1NabR%2BOtOl7Pz6BoUMUXnlEUHUqQjKT%2F%2FY0KCYnO1dlqz6ny%2FTq3%2FAmbPqPL8nxxPF8%2BRoCoOApj3OYYLcEIfNZ26LhKCx4BCuqDbPxNUxHpKFq58guKhHgNR%2F7WKNz0zLDEUNcer8qS1t87%2Ft%2FY0H%2FdvOH9oXz1MFLVhvfos5gGUpoiFRl1dVaL%2FB3Ah8eI5446OIhd5JVITXx1ER1sGCfS4NE03%2BOn7ZB8YsGgu6tOd0aHtletgUr4iXbTvg%3D%3D','Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(12,'Eurasian Heritage Centre: Singapore Attraction','139 Ceylon Road','Located along Ceylon Road, the Eurasian Heritage Centre celebrates the unique culture of Singapore’s Eurasian community. Through personal stories, traditional recipes, and artifacts, it traces the community’s mixed European-Asian ancestry and contributions to Singapore’s development. The center also hosts cultural workshops and serves as a gathering point for preserving this vibrant heritage.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.eurasians.org.sg/','Tuesday to Sunday, 9am â€“ 6pm, Closed on Monday','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340720839_Eurasian_Heritage_Centre%3A_Singapore_Attraction?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=O%2FgT%2BgDlBLJ46t5KGhhCuaAwVyLZzISauOlpe057J78vo9VRG7d5Gn6TY56HSBbb5Xw0EqWN52NygxDofb0w0VYQ1%2BdX6tCK91LQWB02oWC0viz4UC4UVj3woAppiyrXhXWRkxTm4GgGzrhcHShPHmjyq0KFlu9RMplLipconXObu6dzTTneT6Z36LyCWRc%2FotS27VBNl1FnfI2M6mSeQPx6gDHFPr9dYcumeh5UKRfStiig6hwhxo0JRsUxRLb4twj86oPnA3zrwbPNCBX1srFdhXJDttO7X9ImIkVzcQhWI8myZXyHb9tybMg%2B1ORBMuDzN0D4QQMk5a0hy6JBjw%3D%3D','General Singapore\r'),(13,'Shophouses','0','Singapore’s iconic shophouses are a fusion of local and colonial architectural influences, typically featuring narrow façades, ornate tiles, and five-foot walkways. Built between the 1840s and 1960s, they once served as both homes and businesses. Today, many have been restored into boutique cafes, offices, and galleries, symbolizing Singapore’s blend of old and new.','General',2,'','','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340732938_Shophouses?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=N%2FvlguUclbqM%2BJ%2F8OilrkkOsmcL%2BDlCQ754b7edFKkaiqHQgYazVZPZf%2FQfrw97C05eHBvt%2BjCZX%2FCQOCMY8rcpL6kVcPeDTAlBK5%2FcWcLbhHsUa27k6FN077sav%2BaA815uaQUnq6qgCxl6Z8LzUYaznfp%2F54T85ULUeaZ28AsBlW8KjGI202msP0hPuuLM41pscB0mz6gf00v1NJriwcbyyKhZ3RNvRS%2FM1BC4CDplYCPnaTQURDIMtmzli4a0qVecJPXW%2BlyOG1Nd59wlvpiQkSiO8dO34PO%2FT6F%2Fu3t7U7vGAwzAsSB1mA8tAsGXPxbxhPh11gQyjQ0E6rUEXNw%3D%3D','General Singapore\r'),(14,'Capitol Building Singapore','11 Stamford Road','A grand neoclassical landmark from the 1930s, the Capitol Building once housed luxury shops and a cinema. Following restoration, it forms part of the integrated Capitol Singapore complex, which combines heritage with contemporary retail and hospitality. Its façade and art-deco interiors recall a glamorous chapter of Singapore’s urban history.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.capitolsingapore.com','','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340743503_Capitol_Building_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=oz6ESv9DFDp1UsLAHNc2qVgk6k3%2FdrE9l9t5AWc%2BjavoCPXM0j8uJjFNWoJrbVHc0hyMQYMHUCQq%2FPThlCCXUYGEynhTJPQKwSqko1tSV5y%2FTYEvSO6w1Zk33XgGXKtjdC2JTLboHgsFUykxYjCicQKqhwhIBLPbCNQYF5Bo5P%2FM7GNKU1h9Ils5BR13yJoeEXtkBupZIB8EJ7ct0n%2FmLVlClEO1SDRBpJ2zfRX3l7Y3Dp1OKN40TMewzSoIKLnu%2FQh9rXtESzgQjGrfMEkyc%2F%2BzsFUDdx%2Fgeate1fvtoYI%2BYnLDS%2BkZuqiTmDelNMtasGz8omGs9xpQ67SMOgd0sg%3D%3D','Orchard / Bras Basah\r'),(15,'Esplanade Theatre','1 Esplanade Drive','Nicknamed “The Durian” for its spiky exterior, Esplanade – Theatres on the Bay is Singapore’s premier performing arts center. It hosts world-class concerts, plays, and dance performances across multiple venues. Situated along Marina Bay, it also features free outdoor shows and exhibitions, making it a vibrant cultural hub for both locals and international visitors.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.esplanade.com','','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340751999_Esplanade_Theatre?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=UFr0LB0iQgk6p7fSgY43WyMWLFatD7CtRY0WzRxjHkKSsFtSUWpTGmFfkSAy6Cnko1Zz0pJxJMP%2BcSUYlM4%2F4x5CeYhG5BXd8LKkTQYbp7bv4QLvx%2BP8857nPwT7GbPRDnyNBJ3U1L48%2FmcW%2FXzaMhpD6go7XTLMklph6UtmHEja%2FFClXswhYjVOjizezhC8hZa%2Bq1UgMVLVncdso%2Ftnc%2B3iX4pxMB%2FWrANA3rO39HmI6U1B6WCtzRcLfIpSG8eYcP9edvxR1CoylyvcBnXr%2B5N34UfKgCWgTkK5Ha9tFb6UVxPeAeFsomN3qOLsVwSI6XW3nlZu3lQZro8YMFuLsw%3D%3D','Marina Bay\r'),(16,'Gardens by the Bay ','18 Marina Gardens Drive','Gardens by the Bay is a world-renowned horticultural attraction featuring futuristic Supertrees, climate-controlled conservatories, and themed gardens showcasing global flora. Opened in 2012, it represents Singapore’s vision of a “City in a Garden.” The Cloud Forest and Flower Dome are must-see highlights, blending sustainability, technology, and nature in breathtaking harmony.','Parks & Nature',2,'http://www.gardensbythebay.com.sg','Outdoor Gardens: 5am â€“ 2am,Conservatories: 9am â€“ 9pm,OCBC Skyway: 9am â€“ 9pm','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340761842_Gardens_by_the_Bay_?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=U6NzTi6HV%2B7mSmJWFy%2BSSNJ6Un%2BZWIacDjjuw6sueTwq%2BLHa7GoMMKKqBLWL3JyDCsskqzObTlhh8z9AuORHp632dAjQpj%2Fm8iONmdYar2dyVWjyMdsMtbEX%2B4PcgCvOwluHhlpyJW317C2sTTsBauv2CzLNl8yeLiC%2FT8eVKzTNwPIe1%2FAHLVAQMWa8TdxUdcP%2FQCVj3v8kQ6FAhpxyThg6RFjHTN%2F7Y%2BS1lxI86GwHpbFY16RXdTHvEu4oOu099cZbq8uL7RubAWnexVGCT%2BjbBqR01SVj54QLqSOg3Xqpf00mEkrpzmesksN43xiH%2BHOdowi1pZ56vw%2Bz0PKxkw%3D%3D','Marina Bay\r'),(17,'The Interlace ','180-226 Depot Road','The Interlace is an award-winning residential complex designed by architect Ole Scheeren. Its 31 stacked blocks form a hexagonal network resembling interlocked terraces. This revolutionary design promotes community living, natural ventilation, and greenery integration. It’s celebrated globally as a model for sustainable urban architecture.','Architecture & Urban Development',2,'http://www.theinterlace.com','','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340778515_The_Interlace_?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=RrdmPQFqftrbBLuzX4iMKMVt%2FcDUl5DWmEeBs7AHcHSQNFoAW%2FFjIrTGylb1z%2BdsyPOOx0K9b1etYzFShGqV9YlrtUMCtrsT1Z0VkHLWSSgJWZ%2FtyAScOwvhLK5a%2FNWNV%2BJz0qtqcF2bZmpWaexc%2BLpSyDQfU7G5CMg03iqKDAUxOfk3Ht%2BmKkSUn7h9hjSoes%2Fqn%2F26ppGG2FEjHbzHT%2BEKEQjHXE81IYQ%2B7rYrzGxGZWmF8rOltzf1YlKo3gD8TrjndrOBorqogTYYC5c%2FOaC2aXH0mu5RRhdVwdTbfbM2t3cgKs2%2B9TR1THfwltrHhvWYf2sgeO2ILSLkLyC7BA%3D%3D','General Singapore\r'),(18,'PARKROYAL on Pickering','3 Upper Pickering Street','This “hotel in a garden” redefines urban hospitality with cascading sky gardens and energy-efficient design. Located near Chinatown, PARKROYAL on Pickering features lush greenery covering half its total area. Its award-winning architecture blends nature with modern luxury, offering guests panoramic views and eco-friendly living amid the cityscape.','Landmarks & Architecture',1,'http://www.parkroyalhotels.com/en/hotels-resorts/singapore/pickering.html','','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340789784_PARKROYAL_on_Pickering?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=GgQzVuYqBgxpUPuN0WQzK84qBxv02CHU8zkKToldcqDPWytbk8NWyiMo2jCxgHQEAISurkBNWCttKHCB1qaWWkn9lv1nnrkdot4dQNt2oFx%2BPbZleuKGKweBupI%2B6eThoHpwfIX0TQU8Zvvh1k5h4yjpgyntAZJ8fkq1arM%2FM8yPLb4eEyGUAHdBjWwGm6ubLOQAEyppOtC%2FihpfBzZxC%2BGmQ3JLu2HpuP%2FO%2BeFGO87qcGPVaLiA8YfpWJx3Qj0v0BoUXIwCMikjJ8CzQLCpdgySATga9HmKlMdgBPnIkw01koJTexfNJQRx61YjAbMhgw%2FBZfQkKb7gJho%2BMzW4dA%3D%3D','General Singapore\r'),(19,'Lasalle College of the Arts','1 McNally Street','A leading contemporary arts institution in Singapore, LASALLE College of the Arts is recognized for its bold architecture and creative programs. Its open-concept campus, designed by RSP Architects, encourages collaboration across disciplines like fine arts, design, and performing arts. The campus doubles as a public art space featuring student and faculty exhibitions.','Landmarks & Architecture',1,'https://www.lasalle.edu.sg','Administration Office, Monday to Friday, 9am to 6pm. Visitors can still walk around the campus after office opening hours','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340803007_Lasalle_College_of_the_Arts?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=Vzx5zjHSe53Yvp%2FZ7vMxau7McfSd7HLnQ6inleX2b%2FITZWrpxvHTYO2Z%2FhzPth0gWfW8P1OqZM9%2B4vN%2FkyWOzbCu2jU6cww8HdC6q3mwkzfg054pE58K3cZB2cVJRq%2BD%2F1Kf4yha%2Bfr%2B5gSXneI%2BenuS9SFlLPtYldJ9L0a%2FzjIOgmiZ2NTH%2F2W7%2FuL5inJ837UZZ0%2BryluGDkZ3HjVruRxDvahUvYufvdIlUfUFiGuykn3TyAEXGyxvbJ%2B0rR8h%2BS7n%2FutZWEyclOSmrh%2BcYnrKfiXwyXxrr1acBvJLig29GyH7QMCzEdeSDuCpwXKXqkKkpL5gNvVn1fp1bmvHfQ%3D%3D','Central / Civic District\r'),(20,'Kranji War Memorial Landmark in Singapore','9 Woodlands Road','Overlooking the serene Kranji countryside, the Kranji War Memorial honors over 24,000 Allied soldiers who died during World War II. The white headstones and towering central cross stand in solemn tribute to those who gave their lives. Managed by the Commonwealth War Graves Commission, it remains a moving site of remembrance.','Historical & War Memorials',1,'http://bit.ly/19MZ0SB','Daily, 8am â€“ 6.30pm','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340813826_Kranji_War_Memorial_Landmark_in_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=tH67hD%2BLcFVxW070ySWmOzqIt0ZEJB9E91rfuboe0W8%2BHQ48kAYZq4CFVqWq7w3ktIcoWJhRnKXTI%2FVWDXrLrXPAOl06h7CEiQsTJZcZx2QdzXXt%2F0UafwBNrQ9ASJvPWakYSwbdeOiQGFQNdUb0yjwOlN6iLWfCYlZWaxe8w8Nc2SDKjJOQj4V9PHpeSKMPIZCL5G7VfNvBhHPGuob36iqD3o3d5wyCDM88CRbEJzTPpx9LloQSarFQxm4xS%2FZ6fvkNaM8y%2FRSbAIDSENS1xGHIFJY7XbppOTYR5htC3E%2FLXQkAeTxPGtr3qNTQkDQDOw8ymFyWZNy3t5rZsaqHCg%3D%3D','Mandai / Northern\r'),(21,'Indian National Army (INA) Monument in Singapore','Esplanade Park, 0','The INA Monument commemorates the soldiers of the Indian National Army who fought for India’s independence during World War II. Originally built in 1945, the monument was reconstructed to honor their courage and ideals. Located near the Padang, it reflects Singapore’s complex wartime history and ties to regional liberation movements.','Historical & War Memorials',1,'http://www.nhb.gov.sg/places/historic-sites/indian-national-army-memorial','Daily','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340825550_Indian_National_Army_%28INA%29_Monument_in_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=SromyxQYFjYkU8qVHqDqYVrUOu11O6vsisDysEgyxwF9DiUYNosMZF5V92RzM3Dne9rYTrHgphFZCPw6c%2F9UB%2FYS82b6eTwgtJo3KSo9DdD1bsiwotaUpue2%2FfzLFP9WKCH0n7aWpy4pNXbfzOIzefdrCF1OnQBjFvVP6Xcavq6fLaWRUuhMW4iFT7DrgHuM5TwIe%2BDz0Yib9Uavrw47W5aZWCqOYWGys0z6Lcf9Zyh4%2FFh2RYMKOM84ipF1v33hKNsFEtq%2B1n9LAJ9AQ%2FHe7yez5y6gnYcbFM8TzZ5Zznz8TH%2BEA19a3Fd2QUh%2BldIL1JTtkQT6nxqS5MMzKWVNGg%3D%3D','General Singapore\r'),(22,'Reflections at Bukit Chandu: Singapore War Memorial ','31-K Pepys Road','Situated on a hill in Pasir Panjang, Reflections at Bukit Chandu is a heritage center dedicated to the Malay Regiment’s heroic last stand in 1942. Through exhibits, films, and artifacts, it tells the story of bravery and sacrifice during the Battle of Pasir Panjang. The colonial bungalow setting adds poignant authenticity.','Memorials & War History',3,'http://www.nhb.gov.sg/museums/reflections-at-bukit-chandu','Tuesday to Sunday, 9am â€“ 5.30pm,Closed on Monday (except on public holidays)','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340833183_Reflections_at_Bukit_Chandu%3A_Singapore_War_Memorial_?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=EZtkbkW8yo%2B5eNtEg5Bq13bSxnZMeaoZCfI51a0HoJ%2BiUgs6KgmZJEp%2BJKN0skEE1C6zkX4GpEzgdvArTmFYKYE%2BKZxMsosWj9ddWxEN2iBPASWl72nfAB%2B%2BdgSnElLkoGos3CVKKOjIoa3fEO32qMaGipKKjWH%2FZfGMf4PTgMn8XV6pKT9JcH9sEWgolygb5uiJCoYKp%2BrbkGk0nVwLmQsuF%2F8RWbJ3CwgnrU0VTqFm9PwUxpXXv1akHHloI9JHz0xfykD9EthQ16KXnkMz71v5LbfIKtnto%2F0gl3%2F4INySoDyuqDKPuAA%2F%2F9FBCdJRhGHb%2BqckwQpioFRhuKAcrw%3D%3D','General Singapore\r'),(23,'The Cenotaph - a Singapore War Memorial Landmark','Esplanade Park, Connaught Drive, 0','Located at Esplanade Park, The Cenotaph honors Singaporean and Malayan soldiers who perished in the World Wars. Completed in 1922, the granite monument bears the inscription “They Died That We Might Live.” It remains a site for annual remembrance ceremonies and a symbol of collective gratitude.','Historical & War Memorials',1,'http://www.nhb.gov.sg/places/sites-and-monuments/national-monuments/esplanade-park-memorials','Daily','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340844275_The_Cenotaph_-_a_Singapore_War_Memorial_Landmark?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=V5eSOUzjXJ9uZJZU0TjBBFjNrRkJ8YCVWGJfnMgkR9VMM%2BtvA32cYa7Tv1f70v%2Fv1gqu5OASGUgyxf48kyMWPQ6rvu4Kp73Gxypx3Ivs2xBdnteBUGtK3SC6xnhvizCDC0RjsUqH6Ufa8pQRa8Vz6ChqYZpwU9oZjBGnyMLt6E4Zc5scFOqkKNNmlhuF%2F%2BUMoeQj5dHq6dKqx0aQuC6LVAkdhk1%2BJCFjHgx1iue5JmfmXZjX5wzIvxn9NT0ZyYXQjZ6jsxzlEch2ICappd9s6Zpr1xAt1hpajfOApZRtKxGVyZfmsyx5iJMyrt6LjXlE5LRcixHXPpUM745%2BTDEjTw%3D%3D','General Singapore\r'),(24,'The Civilian War Memorial park in Singapore','Bras Basah Road & Beach Road intersection','The Civilian War Memorial, with its four soaring columns, symbolizes unity among Singapore’s ethnic groups who suffered during the Japanese Occupation. Located at Beach Road, it’s often called “The Chopsticks” by locals. The monument houses remains of unknown war victims, serving as a solemn reminder of peace and resilience.','Historical & War Memorials',1,'http://www.nhb.gov.sg/places/sites-and-monuments/national-monuments/civilian-war-memorial','Daily','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340858193_The_Civilian_War_Memorial_park_in_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=g6tq2VJ3mlO6j3k%2B23RtPzDfFQ%2F9isTmVXBR5FnPaDqRG8gu1cz8XLhiSqf7FCVnA3jrOh283HeKFWKpJrlcEx2NVZVOdl%2FTYsTZCINyRhb%2BRlkGVqRP3hInqm3qeicaeqRCTV%2F771O10E5CLRJ%2Fu5wlcqKv8kLhyLIM%2BGW3KrJy9i3MgOfqV0FvrMImlmPN3jGVw8LhDOU0aNyoBuhYzR758uHcRNuJVVyBKXPpDCCwckay22kwBveVjhI9FCKYlehkhzMlJ9z4LmTHBwjFmB9cmNwyONBIwzzHRV7vvlDElRS4BtzFRAethp%2B7JuD6zQU2iCxtkUfym%2BSN%2Fm21FQ%3D%3D','Orchard / Bras Basah\r'),(25,'Sri Veeramakaliamman Temple: Hindu Temple in Singapore','141 Serangoon Road','Located in Little India, Sri Veeramakaliamman Temple is dedicated to the fierce goddess Kali. Established in the mid-19th century, it’s one of Singapore’s oldest Hindu temples. Its vividly decorated towers and statues depict scenes from Hindu mythology. The temple remains a central place of worship and cultural expression for the Indian community.','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'http://www.sriveeramakaliamman.com/','Daily, 5.30am â€“ 9.30pm','https://storage.googleapis.com/sgtourismbored.firebasestorage.app/attractions/1764340868932_Sri_Veeramakaliamman_Temple%3A_Hindu_Temple_in_Singapore?GoogleAccessId=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc%40sgtourismbored.iam.gserviceaccount.com&Expires=4076668800&Signature=TPjH23iB%2BdmX2cn5jacpTNCQuOg26J2FWxdhVHZBjp9M8nVR49h1LeFCOp2tpxrh5rRuxWPRnc9M1HQ7m6wJ8QiltMR9DDHaNc%2FFtcrTrpJpU15oRFtYobzhd5JztkIa7aN8oPFgblv%2BVgmUCDzMAREhVOxuJyG992Td1JrARjnEC2HeL6%2BN4K2o25FaS%2B4DIrYaBT9L29XdOoeEMvdy7ua47ZTYcCcgWAOFa5xjiVHO%2FQ1jrUA6bfsROaTwsro18v%2FYK1NyOf18Z8hccKErua1a5ixx4KxA30ZEKuaa2peUHRtrpGRKCDtOhGa1n1IWsHfd0uaZheSFTCOhk26I%2Bg%3D%3D','Little India\r'),(26,'Maghain Aboth Synagogue','24/26 Waterloo Street','Built in 1878, the Maghain Aboth Synagogue is the oldest Jewish synagogue in Southeast Asia. Its architecture blends neoclassical and colonial styles, featuring large arched windows and a serene courtyard. Still active today, it stands as a symbol of Singapore’s multicultural and multi-faith harmony.','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'http://www.singaporejews.com','Visits are available only by appointment. For more information, email jewishwb@singnet.com.sg or call +65 6337 2189 (ext. 103)',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(27,'Old Supreme Court Singapore','1 St Andrew\'s Road','Completed in 1939, the Old Supreme Court is an architectural masterpiece with Corinthian columns, domed rotunda, and elegant murals. Today, it forms part of the National Gallery Singapore complex. Once the highest seat of justice, it’s now repurposed to preserve and exhibit Singapore’s artistic and judicial heritage.','Historical & War Memorials',1,'http://app.supremecourt.gov.sg/','Expected to open in 2015',NULL,'Central / Civic District\r'),(28,'SIT Flats Singapore','Seng Poh Road and Tiong Bahru Road, 0','Built in the 1930s by the Singapore Improvement Trust (SIT), these flats were among the first public housing developments in the country. Designed to alleviate overcrowding and improve urban hygiene, they laid the foundation for modern public housing that later evolved under the HDB. Today, surviving SIT flats are architectural relics—simple, functional blocks that reflect Singapore’s early efforts at urban modernization and community living.','Architecture & Urban Development',2,'','',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(29,'Lau Pa Sat Singapore','18 Raffles Quay','Lau Pa Sat, meaning “Old Market” in Hokkien, is a historic Victorian cast-iron structure that dates back to 1894. Once a bustling wet market, it’s now a lively food center known for its satay street and local hawker cuisine. By day, the market’s intricate metalwork and colonial charm attract visitors; by night, it transforms into a vibrant open-air dining destination at the heart of Singapore’s Central Business District.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.laupasat.biz','Daily, 24 hours',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(30,'Malay Heritage Centre, Singapore','85 Sultan Gate','Located in the former Istana Kampong Gelam, once the royal seat of Malay sultans in Singapore, the Malay Heritage Centre showcases the rich history and culture of the Malay community. Through interactive exhibits, traditional costumes, and multimedia displays, it tells stories of heritage, faith, and migration. Surrounded by landscaped gardens, the center is a key cultural anchor of the Kampong Glam district.','Cultural Districts & Heritage',2,'http://www.malayheritage.org.sg','Malay Heritage Centre Museum, Tuesday to Sunday, 10am â€“ 6pm, Closed on Monday, Malay Heritage Centre Compound, Tuesday to Sunday, 8am â€“ 8pm, Friday and Saturday, 8am â€“ 10pm, Closed on Monday',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(31,'Peranakan Museum Singapore: History & Culture','39 Armenian Street','The Peranakan Museum explores the hybrid culture of the Peranakans—descendants of Chinese settlers who blended local Malay and colonial influences. Set within a beautifully restored colonial school building, the museum displays exquisite beadwork, porcelain, textiles, and wedding regalia. Its galleries illuminate the Peranakan identity, traditions, and their lasting impact on Singaporean heritage.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://peranakanmuseum.org.sg/','Saturday to Thursday, 10am â€“ 7pm, Friday, 10am â€“ 9pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(32,'Raffles Singapore','1 Beach Road','Opened in 1887, Raffles Hotel is an enduring icon of colonial elegance and luxury. Its white façade, tropical courtyards, and grand verandas have hosted writers, royalty, and film stars. The hotel is also home to the world-famous Singapore Sling cocktail. After extensive restoration, Raffles continues to embody timeless hospitality and old-world charm in the heart of the city.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.raffleshotel.com','Daily, 24 hours',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(33,'Gillman Barracks â€“ YourSingapore ','9 Lock Road','Originally a British military camp built in the 1930s, Gillman Barracks has been transformed into a contemporary arts enclave. Its colonial-era buildings now house international galleries, artist studios, and cafes surrounded by lush greenery. The site regularly hosts art walks, residencies, and public programs, making it a cornerstone of Singapore’s thriving art scene.\n','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'https://www.gillmanbarracks.com/','Tuesday to Saturday, 11am - 7pm, Sunday, 11am - 6pm , Galleries closed on Monday and public holidays.',NULL,'General Singapore'),(34,'ArtScience Museum Singapore','6 Bayfront Ave','A lotus-shaped architectural marvel by Marina Bay Sands, the ArtScience Museum merges technology, design, and innovation. It features world-class traveling exhibitions on science, art, and digital creativity—ranging from interactive installations to immersive multimedia shows. The museum’s sustainable design and futuristic form make it one of Singapore’s most photographed landmarks.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.marinabaysands.com/museum.html','Daily, 10am â€“ 7pm, Last admission at 6pm',NULL,'Marina Bay\r'),(35,'SAM at 8Q & Singapore Art Museum','8 Queen Street','The Singapore Art Museum (SAM) is dedicated to contemporary Southeast Asian art, featuring experimental exhibitions and public engagement programs. Its extension, SAM at 8Q, occupies a former Catholic school building that now hosts dynamic installations and performance art. Together, they reflect Singapore’s commitment to supporting progressive and thought-provoking artistic expression.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'','Monday to Thursday, 10am â€“ 7pm, Friday, 10am â€“ 9pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(36,'Jamae Mosque (Masjid Chulia) Singapore','218 South Bridge Road','Located along South Bridge Road, Jamae Mosque—also known as Masjid Chulia—is one of Singapore’s oldest mosques. Built by Tamil Muslim traders in the 1830s, it combines South Indian and neoclassical architectural influences. The mosque’s green façade and minarets are a familiar sight in Chinatown, symbolizing harmony among diverse faiths in the district.','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'http://www.masjidjamaechulia.sg/','Daily',NULL,'Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(37,'Institute of Contemporary Arts Singapore','LASALLE College of the Arts, 1 McNally Street','Part of LASALLE College of the Arts, the Institute of Contemporary Arts Singapore (ICAS) showcases experimental and research-driven art from local and international artists. Its exhibitions often explore social issues, technology, and cross-disciplinary collaboration. ICAS serves as a bridge between academic inquiry and the wider art community, nurturing critical discourse in the region’s creative landscape.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'https://www.lasalle.edu.sg/ICAS/About-ICAS','Tuesday to Sunday, 12pm - 7pm, Closed on Monday and public holidays',NULL,'Central / Civic District\r'),(38,'Singapore Flyer','30 Raffles Avenue','Standing at 165 meters, the Singapore Flyer is one of the world’s tallest observation wheels, offering panoramic views of Marina Bay, the Central Business District, and beyond. Opened in 2008, each air-conditioned capsule provides a serene 30-minute ride with multimedia guides highlighting key landmarks. It’s a favorite spot for tourists seeking breathtaking cityscapes.','Landmarks & Architecture',1,'http://www.singaporeflyer.com','Daily, 8.30am - 10.30pm, (last admission: 10.15pm)',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(39,'Marina Bay Sands ®','10 Bayfront Avenue','Marina Bay Sands is Singapore’s most recognizable integrated resort, known for its three-tower structure crowned by the iconic SkyPark. The complex includes a luxury hotel, casino, convention center, shopping mall, and the ArtScience Museum. Its infinity pool on the 57th floor offers one of the most stunning views in the world, redefining modern luxury and architectural innovation.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.marinabaysands.com','SkyPark<supÂ® Operating Hours - Monday to Thursday, 9:30am â€“ 10pm, Friday to Sunday, 9:30am â€“ 11pm, Shoppes Daily, 10am â€“ 10pm (hours vary by store)',NULL,'Marina Bay'),(40,'Marina Barrage','8 Marina Gardens Drive','Marina Barrage is a dam and recreational space that plays a vital role in Singapore’s water management strategy. It creates a freshwater reservoir, controls flooding, and doubles as a leisure spot for picnics, kite-flying, and skyline photography. The Green Roof provides an eco-friendly vantage point for viewing Marina Bay’s evolving landscape.','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'https://www.pub.gov.sg/marinabarrage','Marina Barrage - All day  Information Counter Daily, 9am â€“ 9pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(41,'Henderson Waves Bridge','Henderson Road, 0','At 36 meters above Henderson Road, Henderson Waves is Singapore’s highest pedestrian bridge. Its distinctive wave-like wooden structure curves gracefully through the Southern Ridges trail. The bridge’s illuminated panels glow at night, offering a tranquil walkway that blends engineering elegance with panoramic nature views.','Landmarks & Iconic Sites',1,'http://www.nparks.gov.sg','Daily, 24 hours',NULL,'Henderson / Mount Faber\r'),(42,'Pinnacle@Duxton ','1 Cantonment Road','At 36 meters above Henderson Road, Henderson Waves is Singapore’s highest pedestrian bridge. Its distinctive wave-like wooden structure curves gracefully through the Southern Ridges trail. The bridge’s illuminated panels glow at night, offering a tranquil walkway that blends engineering elegance with panoramic nature views.','Architecture & Urban Development',2,'http://www.pinnacleduxton.com.sg','50th storey Skybridge, Daily, 9am â€“10pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(43,'Hajjah Fatimah Mosque ','4001 Beach Road','Built in the 1840s, Hajjah Fatimah Mosque was commissioned by a wealthy Malay businesswoman of the same name. Its eclectic design combines Islamic and European influences, earning it the nickname “the leaning tower of Singapore” due to its slightly tilted minaret. The mosque stands as a tribute to early women entrepreneurs and architectural fusion.','Religious Sites & Temples',2,'','Daily, 9am-9pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(44,'Haw Par Villa: Singapore Theme Park','262 Pasir Panjang Road','Founded in 1937 by the Aw brothers—creators of Tiger Balm—Haw Par Villa is an open-air cultural park filled with vivid dioramas depicting Chinese folklore, mythology, and moral tales. Its most famous exhibit, the “Ten Courts of Hell,” illustrates Buddhist afterlife beliefs. The park remains a unique and eccentric window into traditional Chinese storytelling.','Entertainment & Theme Parks',7,'','Daily, 9am - 7pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(45,'NUS Baba House - Singapore Peranakan Architecture ','157 Neil Road','Managed by the National University of Singapore, the Baba House is a meticulously restored 19th-century Peranakan townhouse on Neil Road. It preserves the domestic interior of a Straits Chinese family, complete with antique furniture, ceramics, and decorative arts. Visitors experience authentic Peranakan architecture and household traditions in an intimate setting.','Cultural Districts & Heritage',2,'http://www.nus.edu.sg/museum/baba','Visits are by appointment only.Visitors must sign up in advance for heritage tours which fall on:Monday, 2pm â€“ 3pm,Tuesday, 6.30pm â€“ 7.30pm,Thursday, 10am â€“ 11am,Saturday, 11am â€“ 12pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(46,'Hong San See Temple Singapore','31 Mohamed Sultan Road','Constructed in 1908 by Chinese migrants from Nan’an, Fujian, Hong San See Temple is dedicated to Guang Ze Zun Wang, the God of Fortune. Its intricate wooden carvings and vibrant roof ornaments exemplify traditional southern Chinese architecture. The temple’s restoration earned it a UNESCO Asia-Pacific Heritage Award for outstanding conservation.','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'','Daily',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(47,'Chinese Heritage Centre, Singapore','12 Nanyang Drive','Located at Nanyang Technological University, the Chinese Heritage Centre explores the experiences of Chinese communities around the world. Through exhibits, oral histories, and research, it highlights migration patterns, cultural adaptation, and identity formation. The building itself, Yunnan Garden’s former administration block, is a national monument symbolizing academic and cultural dialogue.','Cultural Districts & Heritage',2,'http://chc.ntu.edu.sg/','Monday to Friday, 9.30am â€“ 5pm,Saturday and Sunday, 10am â€“5pm, Closed on public holidays',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(48,'Kampong Glam & Malay Culture in Singapore','3 Muscat Street','Kampong Glam is a historic district that once housed Malay royalty and remains a cultural enclave of the Malay and Muslim communities. The area’s shophouses, textile stores, and Middle Eastern eateries surround landmarks like the Sultan Mosque and Istana Kampong Gelam. Its lively blend of tradition and modern lifestyle makes it one of Singapore’s most vibrant neighborhoods.','Cultural Districts & Heritage',2,'http://www.kampongglam.org.sg/','',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(49,'Contemporary Arts Center','43 Malan Road','The Contemporary Arts Centre in Singapore serves as a hub for interdisciplinary exhibitions and experimental performances. It provides platforms for emerging and established artists to explore new media, sound, and installation art. The center supports collaborations between local and international creatives, contributing to Singapore’s reputation as a regional arts leader.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.ntu.ccasingapore.org/','Tuesday to Sunday, 12pm â€“ 7pm Friday, 12pm â€“ 9pm , Closed on Monday. Open on public holiday.',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(50,'The Substation, Singapore Contemporary Art Centre','45 Armenian Street','Founded in 1990, The Substation is Singapore’s first independent contemporary arts center. Housed in a former power substation, it became a pioneer venue for alternative art, performance, and social dialogue. Although it has undergone changes over the years, The Substation remains symbolic of Singapore’s grassroots creative energy and freedom of expression.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.substation.org','The Substation Box Office ,Monday to Friday, 12pm - 2pm and 5pm - 8pm.Saturday and Sunday, open an hour prior to weekend showtimes. Closed on public holidays.The Substation Gallery Daily, 12pm â€“ 9pm Closed on public holidays',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(51,'Red Dot Design Museum, Singapore','28 Maxwell Road, Red Dot Traffic','The Red Dot Design Museum celebrates excellence in industrial and product design. Featuring award-winning creations from around the world, it offers visitors an interactive experience with innovative products, prototypes, and design concepts. Housed in a striking red-brick colonial building, it’s also home to design-focused events and a popular weekend market.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://museum.red-dot.sg','Monday - Friday, 11am â€“ 6 pm, Saturday and Sunday, 11am â€“ 8 pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(52,'Singapore Art Museum','71 Bras Basah Road','The Singapore Art Museum focuses on Southeast Asian contemporary art and serves as a platform for dialogue about regional identity, innovation, and cultural exchange. Its collection spans painting, sculpture, photography, and new media. Housed in a restored 19th-century mission school, it merges heritage architecture with cutting-edge creativity.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.singaporeartmuseum.sg','Monday to Thursday, 10am â€“ 7pm, Friday, 10am â€“ 9pm',NULL,'Orchard / Bras Basah\r'),(53,'Esplanade Theatres on the Bay','1 Esplanade Drive','Esplanade – Theatres on the Bay is Singapore’s premier performing arts complex, affectionately nicknamed “The Durian” for its spiky twin domes. Opened in 2002, it houses concert halls, theatres, studios, and outdoor performance spaces. The Esplanade presents over 3,000 shows annually—from symphonies and musicals to local community performances—making it a vibrant hub for global and homegrown talent. Its waterfront promenade and rooftop terrace offer spectacular views of Marina Bay, uniting art, architecture, and city life.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.esplanade.com/index.jsp','Box Office Opening Hours Daily, 12pm â€“ 8.30pm, The Esplanade Box Office will open 1 hour prior to show commencement for shows before 12 noon and will remain open up to half an hour after show commencement for shows starting after 8.30pm.',NULL,'Marina Bay\r'),(54,'National Design Centre, Singapore','111 Middle Road','The National Design Centre is the focal point for Singapore’s design innovation and creative industries. Located in a cluster of restored Art Deco buildings on Middle Road, it serves as a hub for exhibitions, workshops, and collaborations across fields like architecture, fashion, and product design. It’s also home to the DesignSingapore Council, reflecting the nation’s drive to position design as a force for economic and cultural progress.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.designsingapore.org/ndc/About_NDC.aspx','Daily, 9.00am â€“ 9.00pm',NULL,'Orchard / Bras Basah\r'),(55,'Victoria Theatre Singapore','11 Empress Place','Built in 1905 and restored to its neoclassical grandeur, the Victoria Theatre and Concert Hall are among Singapore’s most iconic civic landmarks. The twin halls, joined by a central clock tower, have hosted everything from colonial-era assemblies to modern orchestral performances. Today, it continues to be a beloved venue for classical concerts, theatre productions, and national events, symbolizing the evolution of Singapore’s performing arts scene.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'https://www.nac.gov.sg/arts-spaces/nac-arts-venues','Daily, 10am â€“ 9pm',NULL,'Central / Civic District\r'),(56,'NUS University Museum, Singapore','50 Kent Ridge Crescent, National University of Singapore','The NUS Museum offers a rich collection of Asian art and artifacts that reflect Singapore’s cultural and intellectual heritage. Its galleries span Southeast Asian, Chinese, and Indian collections, alongside contemporary works and student research projects. As part of the National University of Singapore, the museum emphasizes learning, cross-cultural exchange, and critical dialogue through art.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.nus.edu.sg/cfa/museum/','Tuesday to Friday, 10am â€“ 7.30pm,Saturday and Sunday, 10am â€“ 6pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(57,'Sun Yat Sen Memorial Hall','12 Tai Gin Road','This elegant colonial villa in Balestier once served as the Southeast Asian headquarters of Dr. Sun Yat Sen’s revolutionary movement. Today, it’s a national monument dedicated to the founder of modern China and the historical ties between Chinese communities in Singapore and the global revolution. Exhibits include rare photographs, artifacts, and multimedia installations that tell the story of transnational solidarity and reform.','Landmarks & Architecture',1,'http://www.sysnmh.org.sg','Tuesday to Sunday, 10am â€“ 5pm, Closed on Monday',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(58,'Asian Civilisations Museum & Singapore Museum','1 Empress Place','Overlooking the Singapore River, the Asian Civilisations Museum explores the region’s diverse heritage through art and historical objects spanning centuries. Its galleries showcase connections between Asian cultures, trade routes, and religions—from ancient ceramics to intricate textiles and sacred sculptures. The museum embodies Singapore’s role as a crossroads of civilizations.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://acm.org.sg/','Saturday to Thursday, 10am â€“ 7pm, Friday, 10am â€“ 9pm',NULL,'Central / Civic District\r'),(59,'National Museum of Singapore','93 Stamford Road','Established in 1887, the National Museum of Singapore is the nation’s oldest museum and a storyteller of its journey from colony to global city. Through immersive exhibitions, interactive installations, and historical artifacts, it brings the country’s evolution to life. Its neoclassical façade houses modern multimedia galleries, blending heritage and innovation seamlessly.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://nationalmuseum.sg/','Singapore History Gallery Daily, 10am â€“ 6pm,Last admission at 5.30pm.Singapore Living Galleries Daily, 10am â€“ 8pm Free admission from 6pm â€“8pm , Last admission at 7.30pm',NULL,'Orchard / Bras Basah\r'),(60,'The Japanese Cemetery Park','825B Chuan Hoe Avenue','Located in Hougang, the Japanese Cemetery Park is the largest and oldest Japanese burial ground in Southeast Asia. Established in the late 19th century, it serves as the final resting place for early Japanese settlers, soldiers, and civilians. Today, the site is a tranquil memorial garden featuring cherry blossom trees, ornate tombstones, and memorials to those who shaped early Japanese-Singapore relations.','Parks & Nature',2,'','Daily',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(61,'Dalhousie Obelisk, a Singapore Landmark','1 Empress Place','Built in 1850 to commemorate the visit of Lord Dalhousie, Governor-General of India, the Dalhousie Obelisk stands in front of Victoria Theatre as one of Singapore’s earliest monuments. Its slender column, engraved with historical inscriptions, symbolizes Singapore’s role as a key British port in the 19th century. Though modest in scale, it remains an important relic of colonial commemoration.','Landmarks & Architecture',1,'','Daily',NULL,'Central / Civic District\r'),(62,'Raffles Statue â€“ Sir Stamford Raffles Landing Site ','Raffles Landing Site , 59 Boat Quay','Located by the Singapore River, this statue marks the spot where Sir Stamford Raffles is believed to have landed in 1819. The iconic white figure stands against the modern skyline, symbolizing Singapore’s transformation from trading post to metropolis. The original bronze version stands at Empress Place, while this replica captures the founder’s enduring legacy.','Landmarks & Iconic Sites',1,'','Daily',NULL,'General Singapore'),(63,'Singapore Philatelic Museum','23-B Coleman Street','Dedicated to the art of postage and communication, the Singapore Philatelic Museum explores the nation’s history through stamps, letters, and rare postal artifacts. Its creative exhibits make stamp collecting engaging for all ages, connecting visitors to stories of heritage, design, and international exchange. It’s a small but delightful museum that blends nostalgia with education.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://spm.org.sg','Monday, 1pm â€“ 7pm,Tuesday to Sunday, 9.30am â€“ 7pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(64,'Old Ford Factory Singapore Museum','351 Upper Bukit Timah Road','Once the site where the British formally surrendered Singapore to Japan in 1942, the Old Ford Factory now serves as a World War II exhibition center. Managed by the National Archives, it presents firsthand accounts, photos, and artifacts from the Japanese Occupation. The preserved building and its exhibitions remind visitors of resilience, loss, and the human cost of war.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.nas.gov.sg/moff/Home.aspx','Monday to Saturday, 9am â€“ 5.30pm,Sunday, 12pm â€“ 5.30pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(65,'Singapore Coins and Notes Museum & Museum Singapore','40 Pagoda Street, Level 1','This boutique museum traces Singapore’s monetary evolution—from ancient barter trade to modern currency design. Interactive exhibits showcase rare coins, banknotes, and minting processes. Visitors can even press commemorative coins as souvenirs. It’s an educational stop that reveals how money reflects culture, history, and national identity.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.singaporecoinsandnotesmuseum.com/index1.php','Daily, 10am â€“ 8pm',NULL,'Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(66,'Singapore City Gallery','45 Maxwell Road, The URA Centre','Located at the Urban Redevelopment Authority (URA), Singapore City Gallery offers a fascinating look into the city’s urban planning journey. Through large-scale models, data visualizations, and interactive exhibits, visitors can explore how a small island evolved into a sustainable global city. The gallery is both educational and inspiring, revealing the design thinking behind Singapore’s future growth.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.ura.gov.sg/uol/citygallery.aspx','Monday to Saturday, 9am â€“ 5pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(67,'Changi Museum Singapore','1000 Upper Changi Road North','The Changi Museum honors the experiences of prisoners of war and civilians interned during the Japanese Occupation. Through letters, murals, and personal artifacts, it tells stories of endurance, hope, and reconciliation. Adjacent to the museum is a chapel—a simple yet powerful space of remembrance visited by veterans and families from around the world.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.changimuseum.sg','Daily, 9:30am â€“ 5pm,(Last admission: 4.30pm)',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(68,'MINT Museum of Toys Singapore & Toy Museum','26 Seah Street','Home to over 50,000 vintage toys, the MINT Museum of Toys is a nostalgic haven for collectors and dreamers. Its exhibits span from the 1840s to the 1980s, featuring rare collectibles from Batman to Popeye. The museum’s modern design and rooftop bar add to its playful charm, making it a fun destination for both adults and children.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.emint.com','Daily, 9.30am â€“ 6.30pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(69,'Lim Bo Seng Memorial','Esplanade Park, Connaught Drive, 0','Erected in honor of war hero Lim Bo Seng, this pagoda-style memorial stands in Esplanade Park. Lim was a resistance leader who fought valiantly against Japanese occupation and became a symbol of patriotism and sacrifice. The memorial’s design reflects both Chinese and local architectural influences, representing courage across cultures.','Historical & War Memorials',1,'http://www.nhb.gov.sg/places/sites-and-monuments/national-monuments/esplanade-park-memorials','Daily',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(70,'Universal Studios Singapore','8 Sentosa Gateway, Sentosa Island','Located on Sentosa Island, Universal Studios Singapore is Southeast Asia’s first Hollywood-themed park. It features thrilling rides, live shows, and immersive zones based on blockbuster films like Jurassic Park and Transformers. From family-friendly attractions to adrenaline-pumping rollercoasters, it offers a full day of cinematic adventure and entertainment.','Entertainment & Theme Parks',7,'http://www.rwsentosa.com/language/en-US/Homepage/Attractions/UniversalStudiosSingapore','Open daily at 10am.Closing time varies. Please visit the official website for more information.',NULL,'Sentosa'),(71,'Singapore Science Centre','15 Science Centre Road','The Science Centre Singapore is a leading institution for science education and discovery. Through hundreds of interactive exhibits, planetarium shows, and hands-on experiments, it makes complex concepts fun for all ages. The center also hosts the Omni-Theatre and seasonal events, encouraging curiosity and lifelong learning.\n','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.science.edu.sg/Pages/SCBHome.aspx','Science Centre Singapore Daily, 10am â€“ 6pm(last admission: 5.15pm) .Omni Theatre Daily, 12pm â€“ 6pm (last show: 6pm) . Snow City Daily, 10am â€“ 6pm (last admission: 5pm) School and public holidays, 10am â€“ 7pm (last admission: 6pm)',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(72,'Fuk Tak Chi Museum & Places of Interest','76 Telok Ayer Street','Originally a temple built in 1824 by Hakka and Cantonese immigrants, Fuk Tak Chi is now a museum dedicated to early Chinese settlers in Singapore. Its compact galleries display artifacts, maps, and dioramas depicting life in the early trading port. Located along Telok Ayer Street, it’s one of the city’s oldest surviving temples and a testament to Chinese heritage.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'http://www.fareastsquare.com.sg/home/index.aspx?key=fuktakchimuseum','Daily, 10am â€“ 10pm',NULL,'Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(73,'Katong Antique House Singapore: Peranakan Heritage','208 East Coast Road','Katong Antique House is a privately owned museum preserving the legacy of the Peranakan community. Set in a traditional shophouse, it displays intricate porcelain, beadwork, and antique furniture curated by heritage advocate Peter Wee. The space offers an intimate glimpse into the domestic life and customs of Singapore’s Straits Chinese families.','Museums & Art Galleries',3,'','Daily, 11am â€“ 4.30pm',NULL,'Eastern Singapore\r'),(74,'Chinese Garden Singapore','1 Chinese Garden Road','Designed in 1975 by Taiwanese architect Prof. Yuen-chen Yu, the Chinese Garden is a tranquil retreat in Jurong inspired by classical northern Chinese landscaping. Its pagodas, moon gates, and stone bridges evoke imperial gardens, while the adjacent Japanese Garden complements its harmony. It’s a favorite spot for photography and leisurely strolls.\n\n','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'http://www.nhb.gov.sg/resources/virtual-exhibitions-and-tours/walking-through-heritage/chinese-garden','Daily, 6am â€“ 11pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(75,'East Coast Park Singapore','Along East Coast Parkway and East Coast Park Service Road, 0','Stretching over 15 kilometers along Singapore’s southeastern coastline, East Coast Park is the island’s largest beach park. It’s a popular weekend destination for cycling, picnicking, and water sports. With seaside eateries, camping zones, and sunset views, it epitomizes Singapore’s blend of urban convenience and outdoor leisure.\n\n','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'http://www.nparks.gov.sg/cms/index.php?option=com_visitorsguide&task=parks&id=14&Itemid=371','',NULL,'Eastern Singapore\r'),(76,'Fort Canning Park','Bounded by Hill Street, Canning Rise, Clemenceau Avenue and River Valley Road','Fort Canning Park is a historic hilltop green space that has witnessed Singapore’s transformation for centuries—from ancient Malay royal grounds to a colonial fortress. Today, it hosts concerts, art festivals, and heritage trails among lush gardens. Key attractions include the Battle Box, spice garden, and the nine historical gardens reflecting different eras.','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'http://www.nparks.gov.sg/cms/index.php?option=com_visitorsguide&task=parks&id=16Th','',NULL,'Central / Civic District\r'),(77,'Kusu Island, Singapore','Kusu Island','Kusu Island, meaning “Tortoise Island” in Chinese, is a serene offshore sanctuary famous for its temple and Malay shrines. Every year, devotees make pilgrimages to the Da Bo Gong Temple and the Kramat shrines for blessings. The island’s lagoons, beaches, and resident tortoises make it a peaceful escape from the city.\n','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'http://www.sentosa.com.sg/en/nature/southern-islands/kusu-island/','',NULL,'Pulau Ubin / Southern Islands\r'),(78,'Jurong Bird Park, Singapore: Attractions & Things to Do','2 Jurong Hill','Jurong Bird Park, part of the Mandai Wildlife Reserve, was Asia’s largest aviary before its relocation to Bird Paradise in 2023. For decades, it housed thousands of birds across immersive habitats like the Waterfall Aviary and Lory Loft. The park was beloved for its conservation work and educational exhibits on avian diversity.','Nature & Wildlife Parks',5,'http://www.birdpark.com.sg','Daily, 8.30am - 6pm',NULL,'Jurong / West\r'),(79,'HortPark','33 Hyderabad Road','HortPark is a horticultural haven connecting science, sustainability, and recreation. It showcases themed gardens, urban farming projects, and landscape innovation. Visitors can join guided walks, garden therapy sessions, and educational workshops. As part of the Southern Ridges, it promotes green living and biodiversity awareness.','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'http://www.nparks.gov.sg/cms/index.php?option=com_visitorsguide&task=parks&id=17&Itemid=73','Daily, 6am â€“10pm',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(80,'Adventure Cove Waterpark, Singapore','8 Sentosa Gateway, Sentosa Island','Located on Sentosa Island, Adventure Cove Waterpark blends thrilling rides with marine encounters. Guests can zip down high-speed slides, drift along the Adventure River, or snorkel among tropical fish at Rainbow Reef. It’s both a family-friendly attraction and an educational experience emphasizing marine conservation.','Entertainment & Theme Parks',7,'http://www.rwsentosa.com/language/en-US/Homepage/Attractions/MarineLifePark/AdventureCoveWaterpark','Daily, 10am â€“ 6pm',NULL,'Sentosa'),(81,'Lasalle College of the Arts','1 McNally Street','','Landmarks & Architecture',1,'https://www.lasalle.edu.sg','Administration Office Monday to Friday, 9am â€“ 6pm.Visitors can still walk around the campus after office opening hours.',NULL,'Central / Civic District\r'),(82,'Singapore River: History & Culture Tours','10 Eu Tong Sen Street','Once a bustling trade artery, the Singapore River is now lined with restored warehouses, museums, and riverside dining. Cruises along the river trace the city’s journey from colonial port to modern metropolis. Landmarks like Clarke Quay, Boat Quay, and the Cavenagh Bridge preserve the area’s historic charm while embracing vibrant nightlife.','Cultural Districts & Heritage',2,'http://singapore-river.com/','',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(83,'Joo Chiat/Katong: Singapore Peranakan Neighbourhood','113 East Coast Road','Joo Chiat and Katong are heritage neighborhoods known for pastel-colored shophouses, ornate Peranakan tiles, and authentic local cuisine. The area captures the essence of old Singapore, where Eurasian and Peranakan cultures mingle. Visitors can explore traditional bakeries, antique houses, and the famous laksa eateries that define its identity.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'','',NULL,'Eastern Singapore\r'),(84,'Sentosa Island, Singapore: Leisure & Attractions','33 Allanbrooke Road','Sentosa Island is Singapore’s ultimate resort getaway, offering beaches, adventure parks, and luxury accommodations. Once a military fortress, it’s now home to attractions like Universal Studios, S.E.A. Aquarium, and Fort Siloso. Linked to the mainland by cable car and monorail, Sentosa is the perfect mix of fun, relaxation, and coastal beauty.\n\n','Theme Parks & Entertainment',7,'http://www.sentosa.com.sg','',NULL,'Sentosa\r'),(85,'Chinatown Singapore: History & Culture','2 Banda Street','Chinatown is a vibrant district where old temples, market stalls, and modern cafes coexist. Founded in the 19th century for Chinese immigrants, it retains its heritage through landmarks like the Buddha Tooth Relic Temple, traditional medicine halls, and festive street markets. It’s a living museum of culture, faith, and cuisine.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.chinatown.sg/','',NULL,'Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(86,'Buddha Tooth Relic Temple & Museum in Singapore','288 South Bridge Road','This grand Tang-style temple in Chinatown houses what is believed to be a sacred tooth relic of the Buddha. Completed in 2007, it features ornate interiors, giant prayer wheels, and museum galleries showcasing Buddhist art. The temple’s rooftop garden and prayer hall provide moments of serenity amidst the bustling city.','Temples & Religious Sites',1,'http://www.btrts.org.sg/','Daily, 7am â€“ 7pm',NULL,'Chinatown / Clarke Quay\r'),(87,'Labrador Nature Reserve','Labrador Villa Road','Labrador Nature Reserve combines lush coastal forest with World War II history. Walking trails lead past gun emplacements, tunnels, and viewpoints overlooking the sea. It’s part of Singapore’s Southern Ridges and a haven for birdwatchers and history enthusiasts alike, merging nature and heritage seamlessly.','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'http://www.nparks.gov.sg/cms/index.php?option=com_visitorsguide&task=naturereserves&id=48','',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(88,'Marina Bay Singapore: Attractions & Things to do','11 Marina Boulevard','Marina Bay is Singapore’s most iconic district, showcasing cutting-edge architecture, arts, and recreation. Landmarks like Marina Bay Sands, the Merlion, and Gardens by the Bay define the skyline, while waterfront promenades host light shows and festivals. It’s the city’s showcase of innovation and urban beauty.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.marina-bay.sg','',NULL,'Marina Bay\r'),(89,'Marina Bay Sands, Singapore','10 Bayfront Avenue','A global symbol of modern luxury, Marina Bay Sands integrates hospitality, entertainment, and architecture into one breathtaking complex. Its SkyPark offers 360-degree views, while its infinity pool has become a social media icon. With theaters, art installations, and Michelin-starred dining, it exemplifies Singapore’s cosmopolitan spirit.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.marinabaysands.com/index.html','Opening hours varies. Please visit the official website for more information.',NULL,'Marina Bay\r'),(90,'Chek Jawa','Chek Jawa, Pulau Ubin, 0','Located on Pulau Ubin, Chek Jawa is a unique intertidal ecosystem where six distinct habitats meet—mangroves, coral rubble, and seagrass lagoons among them. Boardwalks and observation towers allow visitors to explore the area’s biodiversity without disturbing it. It’s one of Singapore’s richest natural heritage sites, best visited during low tide.','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'https://www.nparks.gov.sg/gardens-parks-and-nature/parks-and-nature-reserves/pulau-ubin-and-chek-jawa/what-to-see/about-chek-jawa','',NULL,'Pulau Ubin / Southern Islands\r'),(91,'Merlion Park, Singapore: Attractions & Things to Do','One Fullerton','Home to the iconic half-lion, half-fish statue, Merlion Park is one of Singapore’s most visited landmarks. Overlooking Marina Bay, the 8.6-meter Merlion symbolizes the city’s origins as a fishing village named “Temasek.” The park offers great photo spots, skyline views, and proximity to other downtown attractions.','Landmarks & Iconic Sites',1,'','',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(92,'Marina Bay Sands ® SkyPark','Level 57, 10 Bayfront Avenue','The SkyPark, perched atop Marina Bay Sands, is a 340-meter-long observation deck offering panoramic city views. Guests can enjoy the infinity pool, restaurants, and sunset vistas stretching across Marina Bay and the Singapore Strait. It’s an unmissable vantage point for visitors seeking the best skyline perspectives.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.marinabaysands.com/sands-skypark.html','Monday to Thursday, 9.30am - 10pm.Friday to Sunday, 9.30am - 11pm',NULL,'Marina Bay'),(93,'Resorts World Sentosa, Singapore ','8 Sentosa Gateway, Sentosa Island','Resorts World Sentosa is a premier integrated resort featuring luxury hotels, casinos, and attractions like Universal Studios and the S.E.A. Aquarium. Opened in 2010, it redefined leisure tourism in Singapore. The resort also champions marine conservation and sustainable hospitality, making it both an entertainment hub and responsible destination.','Theme Parks & Entertainment',7,'http://www.rwsentosa.com','Opening hours differ for the various attractions.',NULL,'Sentosa\r'),(94,'Singapore Garden & Singapore Park','Henderson Road, Mount Faber Park, Telok Blangah Hill Park','Singapore’s gardens and parks network forms the “City in a Garden” vision, integrating greenery into every aspect of urban life. From community parks to grand gardens, they serve as lungs of the city and sanctuaries for biodiversity. Walking and cycling paths connect major green corridors, reflecting Singapore’s dedication to sustainable living.','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'','',NULL,'Henderson / Mount Faber\r'),(95,'Sungei Buloh','301 Neo Tiew Crescent','Sungei Buloh Wetland Reserve is Singapore’s first ASEAN Heritage Park and a paradise for birdwatchers. Its mangrove trails and observation hides provide close encounters with migratory birds, mudskippers, and monitor lizards. It’s an ecological gem showcasing Singapore’s success in conserving natural habitats amid urban growth.\n\n','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'https://www.nparks.gov.sg/sbwr','',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(96,'S.E.A. Aquarium','8 Sentosa Gateway, Sentosa Island','One of the world’s largest aquariums, S.E.A. Aquarium at Resorts World Sentosa houses over 100,000 marine animals across 40 habitats. Its massive Ocean Dome offers mesmerizing views of manta rays and sharks gliding through open waters. Educational exhibits highlight marine conservation and ocean sustainability.\n\n','Entertainment & Theme Parks',7,'http://www.rwsentosa.com/language/en-US/Homepage/Attractions/MarineLifePark','Open daily at 10am.Closing time varies. Please visit the official website for more information.',NULL,'Sentosa\r'),(97,'Singapore Botanic Gardens','1 Cluny Road','A UNESCO World Heritage Site, the Singapore Botanic Gardens is over 160 years old and home to more than 10,000 plant species. Highlights include the National Orchid Garden, Rainforest Trail, and Symphony Lake. It’s both a research center and a beloved recreational spot that embodies Singapore’s deep-rooted love of nature.\n\n','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'http://www.sbg.org.sg','Singapore Botanic Gardens Daily, 5am - 12am National Orchid Garden Daily, 8.30am - 7pm(last ticket sales at 6pm)Jacob Ballas Children\'s Garden Tuesday to Sunday, 8am - 7pm(last admission: 6.30pm)Closed every Monday (except when it falls on a designated public holiday)SBG Heritage MuseumDaily*, 9am - 6pm*Closed every last Monday of the month (except when it falls on a designated public holiday, it will be closed the following day)',NULL,'Central / Civic District\r'),(98,'Singapore Zoo: Attractions & Things to Do','80 Mandai Lake Road','The Singapore Zoo is world-famous for its “open concept” habitats that allow animals to roam freely within naturalistic enclosures. Housing over 300 species, it emphasizes conservation, education, and animal welfare. Visitors can enjoy rainforest walks, feeding sessions, and wildlife shows that inspire appreciation for nature.\n\n','Nature & Wildlife Parks',5,'http://www.zoo.com.sg','Daily, 8.30am â€“ 6pm(last ticket sale at 5.30pm)',NULL,'Mandai / Northern\r'),(99,'River Safari Singapore','80 Mandai Lake Road','Nestled between the Zoo and Night Safari, River Safari (now River Wonders) showcases freshwater ecosystems from around the world. Its star attractions include giant pandas, manatees, and the world’s largest freshwater aquarium. The park combines recreation with education on river conservation and biodiversity.\n\n','Nature & Wildlife Parks',5,'http://www.riversafari.com.sg','Daily, 10am - 7pm',NULL,'Mandai / Northern\r'),(100,'Cable Car Singapore & Mount Faber','109 Mount Faber Road, 0','Singapore’s Cable Car system links Mount Faber to Sentosa Island, offering stunning aerial views of the harbor and skyline. Mount Faber itself is a scenic hilltop park with dining spots and panoramic lookouts. The cable car experience is a blend of nostalgia, sightseeing, and gentle adventure.','Nature & Wildlife Parks',5,'http://www.mountfaber.com.sg/','',NULL,'Henderson / Mount Faber\r'),(101,'Night Safari, Singapore','80 Mandai Lake Road','The world’s first nocturnal zoo, the Night Safari offers immersive tram rides and walking trails that reveal the secret lives of animals after dark. Opened in 1994, it’s home to over 900 animals across 100 species. The park’s conservation mission and unique experience make it one of Singapore’s top attractions.\n\n','Nature & Wildlife Parks',5,'http://www.nightsafari.com.sg','Daily, 7.30pm - 12am .Last ticket sale at 11.15pm.F and B and retail outlets open from 5.30pm',NULL,'Mandai / Northern\r'),(102,'Pulau Ubin & Singapore Islands','Pulau Ubin, 0','Pulau Ubin is a rustic island that preserves the charm of Singapore’s kampong (village) past. Wooden houses, cycling trails, and mangrove boardwalks invite visitors to explore traditional island life. Together with nearby islands like St John’s and Lazarus, it offers a glimpse of Singapore’s natural and cultural roots.\n\n','Nature & Wildlife Parks',5,'http://www.nparks.gov.sg/cms/index.php?option=com_visitorsguide&task=parks&id=29&Itemid=73','',NULL,'Pulau Ubin / Southern Islands\r'),(103,'Little India Singapore: History & Culture','60 Bukit Timah Road','Little India is a vibrant district brimming with color, spirituality, and culture. From flower garland stalls to spice shops and temples, it captures the essence of Singapore’s Indian community. Iconic landmarks include the Sri Veeramakaliamman Temple and Tekka Centre. Festivals like Deepavali bring the streets to life with lights and music.\n\n','Cultural Districts & Heritage',2,'http://www.littleindia.com.sg/','',NULL,'Little India\r'),(104,'Orchard Road, Singapore: Asia\'s Most Famous Shopping Street','437 Orchard Road','Orchard Road is Singapore’s retail epicenter, lined with luxury malls, boutiques, and restaurants. Once a plantation area, it evolved into Asia’s most famous shopping boulevard. Beyond retail, it features art installations, rooftop gardens, and festive light-ups that make it a must-visit urban destination.\n','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.orchardroad.org/','',NULL,'Orchard / Bras Basah\r'),(105,'MacRitchie Singapore & Singapore Nature Reserve','Lornie Rd, Singapore 298735','MacRitchie Reservoir Park is Singapore’s oldest nature reserve and a haven for outdoor enthusiasts. Its hiking trails, canoeing routes, and treetop suspension bridge offer rich encounters with native wildlife. The park also plays a vital role in water conservation and public recreation.\n\n','Parks & Nature Reserves',2,'http://www.nparks.gov.sg/cms/macritchie.php?option=com_visitorsguide&task=naturereserves&id=49&Itemid=75','',NULL,'General Singapore\r'),(106,'Gardens by the Bay','18 Marina Gardens Drive','Gardens by the Bay is Singapore’s flagship nature-park complex, fusing cutting-edge design with living ecosystems. Wander beneath the Supertree Grove—vertical gardens up to 50 m tall—then step onto the OCBC Skyway for skyline views. Inside the cooled conservatories, the Flower Dome showcases Mediterranean and semi-arid landscapes, while the Cloud Forest wraps a misty mountain around one of the world’s tallest indoor waterfalls. Powered by solar harvesting, biomass, and rainwater reuse, the site is a showcase of sustainable engineering. As night falls, the Supertrees glow during the Garden Rhapsody light-and-sound show, turning the bayfront into a luminous canopy. Easily reached via Bayfront MRT, it’s a must-see blend of horticulture, architecture, and innovation.','Parks & Nature',2,'http://www.gardensbythebay.com.sg/en/home.htm','Bay South Outdoor Gardens Daily, 5am - 2am Cooled Conservatories and OCBC Skyway Daily, 9am - 9pm(last ticket sale at 8pm).Far East Organization Children\'s Garden Tuesday* to Friday, 10am - 7pm(last admission: 6.30pm)*Closed on Tuesday if a public holiday falls on the preceding Monday Weekends and public holidays, 9am - 9pm(last admission: 8.30pm)',NULL,'Marina Bay\r'),(107,'Civic District, Singapore','150 North Bridge Road','The Civic District is the historic and cultural heart of Singapore, where the nation’s story began. Centered around the Singapore River and Padang, it’s home to grand colonial landmarks like the National Gallery, Asian Civilisations Museum, and Old Parliament House. Tree-lined boulevards and riverside promenades connect art spaces, memorials, and gardens, blending heritage with modern city life. As the birthplace of Singapore’s government and culture, the Civic District remains a symbolic bridge between the past and present.','Cultural Districts & Heritage',2,'','',NULL,'Central / Civic District\r'),(108,'HarbourFront, Singapore','81 Telok Blangah Road','HarbourFront is Singapore’s southern gateway to the sea, linking the mainland to Sentosa Island and regional ferry routes. The district combines maritime heritage with urban entertainment—featuring VivoCity, the country’s largest mall, and the bustling HarbourFront Cruise Centre. Visitors can enjoy seaside dining, scenic cable car rides, and views of passing ships along Keppel Bay. It’s both a transportation hub and a lively leisure waterfront for shopping and exploration.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.shba.org.sg/','',NULL,'Henderson / Mount Faber\r'),(109,'Bras Basah.Bugis, Singapore Cultural District','220 Victoria Street','The Bras Basah.Bugis district is Singapore’s vibrant arts and heritage quarter, where creativity and history intertwine. Anchored by institutions like the National Museum, Singapore Art Museum, and School of the Arts, it’s a lively precinct filled with galleries, murals, and performance venues. The area’s blend of conserved buildings and contemporary design reflects the city’s evolving creative identity. By day or night, Bras Basah.Bugis pulses with youthful energy, festivals, and artistic expression.','Shopping & Food Streets',2,'http://www.brasbasahbugis.sg/','',NULL,'Orchard / Bras Basah\r');
